From 4a06a353c2651c0bed92c60689f2f9836a89e86a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 08:56:12 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 075/119] scene-referred S3: the ACES matrices, and the fp16
 floor reaches the working space itself

The numbers S3 and S4 are both built on, in one place, plus the one hole S0 left
in the format floor - the target the working space actually lives in.

CommonMath.cfi gains, in the colour-space block:

  REC709_TO_ACESCG / ACESCG_TO_REC709   linear Rec.709 (D65) <-> ACEScg (AP1, D60),
                                        Bradford-adapted, 8 decimals
  Rec709ToACEScg() / ACEScgToRec709()   mul() wrappers
  ACESCG_LUMINANCE_WEIGHTS              the AP1 Y row, sum exactly 1.0
  ACEScgLuminance()
  ACESCCT_* + LinearToACEScct() / ACEScctToLinear()   S4's shaper, scalar and float3

Every digit is an exact double-precision operator parameter read out of OCIO's own
transform by tools/ocio-bake/verify_matrices.py against PyOpenColorIO 2.5
(studio-config-v4.0.0_aces-v2.0_ocio-v2.5), not a sampled float32 processor and not
recalled from memory - four entries of the forward matrix and seven of the inverse
were wrong in the 7th decimal in the research note's first pass, which is why the
comment names the script that regenerates them. These are standards (Rec.709, AP1,
D65, D60, Bradford are each fixed by specification), so there is no parameter in
them and nothing runtime-settable; the way they go wrong is the bake tool and the
shader disagreeing, and that is fixed by one source, not by a cbuffer.

Unlike every transfer function beneath them these deliberately carry no saturate().
The block right below shows why that had to be said out loud: all eight stock
transfer functions open with one, and none of them is usable on scene-referred data.

The broken Convert_REC709toREC2020 at the end of that block stays exactly as it is
and gains a comment saying so - it cannot compile (a missing opening paren), it is
not a matrix multiply even repaired (the commas are the comma operator) and each
row multiplies a different component. Its coefficients are correct, which is what
makes it dangerous. Repairing it would be a stock-path source change with no stock
consumer; the comment points at the working code instead.

Second half: ResolveHDRMainFormat() + UpdateHDRMainFormats(), the same
max(cvar, sceneReferred ? 2 : 0) shape and the same in-place
CTexture::ReformatRenderTarget() path S0 gave the seven satellites, now applied to
$HDRTarget and its three scaled siblings. Those take the high-quality HDR alias and
are already fp16 at the registered default r_HDRTexFormat 1, so this fires on
neither path in practice - but $HDRTarget IS the working space, and "r_HDRTexFormat 0"
would have put the whole scene-referred chain in an unsigned 11:11:10 buffer. 709 ->
AP1 alone produces no negatives, so S3 by itself would survive; S4's CDL and any LMT
can, and then the sign bit matters. All four or none, because the scaled pair feeds
auto exposure, bloom and the shafts.

Build: CryRenderD3D11, CinematicCamera, 0/0.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01A72MFkdSczncE7au9UVnmu
